-- title:
--   Gauss valuation on a finite level field is unramified over A'
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $M'$ a nonzero natural number, and write $F$ for `fieldBar q M'`, the base change to $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ (coefficientwise, inside $\bar{\mathbb Q}((t))$) of the function field of the modular curve of level $q^2M'$ attached to the subgroup `levelH q M'` of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$, namely the kernel of the `ZMod.unitsMap` reduction along `dvd_sq_mul q M'`. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, let $k_0$ be an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q \subseteq \bar{\mathbb Q}$, let $K'$ be an intermediate field of $k_0 \subseteq \bar{\mathbb Q}$ finite over $k_0$, and let $A'$ be a valuation subring of $K'$ consisting exactly of the elements of $K'$ lying in $A$, assumed to be a discrete valuation ring. Regard $F$ as a $k_0$-algebra through $\bar{\mathbb Q}$. Then for every intermediate field $F_0$ of $k_0 \subseteq F$ such that (i) the $k_0$-subfield generated by the image of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ together with $F_0$ is all of $F$; (ii) whenever $c_0,\dots,c_{m-1} \in \bar{\mathbb Q}$ are $K'$-linearly independent and $a_0,\dots,a_{m-1}$ lie in the subfield $L' := k_0(\text{image of }K') \sqcup F_0$ of $F$ with $\sum_i c_i a_i = 0$, all $a_i$ vanish; and (iii) every $f \in F$ whose Laurent series has all coefficients in the image of $\mathbb Q$, i.e. lies in the range of `coeffEmb (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)`, belongs to $F_0$; and for every valuation subring $O$ of $F$ admitting the Gauss presentation over $A$, i.e. $f \in O$ if and only if there are Laurent series $x,y$ with coefficients in $A$ with $y$ nonzero modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ and $f\cdot y = x$ in $\bar{\mathbb Q}((t))$, the following two assertions hold. First, every nonzero $f \in L'$ can be scaled by some nonzero $c \in K'$ so that $cf$ and $(cf)^{-1}$ both lie in $O$, that is, $cf$ is a unit of $O$. Second, for every generator $\varpi'$ of the maximal ideal of $A'$, every $f \in L'$ that is a non-unit of $O$ can be written $f = \varpi' g$ with $g \in L'$ lying in $O$.
--
--   This is the $q$-expansion principle in valuation-theoretic form: the Gauss (sup-norm) valuation of the Igusa frame, restricted to the level field $K'\cdot F_0$ inside the full-level field, has value group equal to that of the constants $K'$, so that it is unramified over $A'$ and $\varpi'$ remains a uniformiser. It is used in the identification of the Igusa Gauss ring with the two-chart integral model at finite level, in particular by the results named `eq_of_forall_coe_levelField_mem_igusaRing_iff_twoChartIntegralModel` and its variants for $q = 2$ and $q = 3$.
